**Proposition 8.1 (exact structural storage).** At any represented cut let
$c$ count actually constructed finite locations in $C$, and let $K$ count
all constructed native tape cells, including the meter. Then

$$
\begin{aligned}
V&=127+2q+4c+48K,\\
L&=64+q+2c+24K,\\
E_{\rm tree}&=V-1,\qquad
E_{\rm access}=\ell_{\rm head}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{CSA.10}
$$

Here $\ell_{\rm head}=65$ after acquisition and is the actual constructed
link count before it. Occupied slots number $2(V-1+\ell_{\rm head})$,
and slot capacity is $7V$. A fresh finite bit adds four vertices and two
terminals; a native cell append adds 48 vertices and 24 terminals.

Proof. Reuse SSA Proposition 4.5's 47-vertex/24-leaf cell and tape counts.
An empty finite socket is one vertex/leaf; replacing it by a five-vertex,
three-leaf switch adds four/two. Hence $R(z)$ has $2q-1+4c$ vertices
and $q+2c$ leaves. Add the outer scaffold and 64 tape components to obtain
CSA.10. Each edge occupies its two endpoint slots. After acquisition the
occupied count is $2V+128$. No cell is subtracted on erasure, retirement,
copy completion or code-version deactivation. Apparatus $\Pi$ is additional
to these carrier counts. ∎

